# Answer 6 · Modernisation, Dependency and the Subordination of Women (CSS 2019)
@py question 2019-4

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Part one explains each theory and its relevance to gender equality; part two uses both to analyse why women remain subordinate in modern societies, and weighs what each misses.
@ask ① EXPLAIN the RELEVANCE of MODERNISATION and DEPENDENCY THEORY to GENDER EQUALITY
@ask ② CRITICALLY ANALYSE the SUBORDINATION of women in MODERN SOCIETIES in their light
(1) Introduction: two rival theories of why nations are poor; each implies a different path to gender equality
(2) Modernisation theory: Rostow's five stages; tradition as the obstacle; Western values as the cure
(3) Its relevance to gender equality: education, cities and media widen women's choices
(4) Its gender critique: Boserup showed men captured land, credit and technology; culture blamed
(5) Dependency theory: centre and periphery; underdevelopment produced by unequal exchange
(6) Its relevance to gender equality: explains cheap female labour, debt and poor services
(7) Its gender critique: gender-blind; ignores local patriarchy and unpaid work
(8) Subordination in modern societies, through modernisation's lens: new patriarchy of the double burden and beauty
(9) Subordination through dependency's lens: women at the bottom of global chains; austerity
(10) What both miss: household power, unpaid care, women's agency
(11) Pakistan and conclusion: culture and global structures together; gender analysis joins them
:::

## Decoding the question
Two limbs. **"Explain the relevance … to gender equality"** asks what each theory **implies** for women's equality — what it helps us see, and what path it suggests. **"Critically analyse the subordination of women in modern societies in the light of these two theories"** asks you to **use** both theories to explain why women remain subordinate even where societies have "modernised" — and to judge what each theory explains and misses.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Modernisation theory** and **dependency theory** are the two classic explanations of why some nations are rich and others poor. Modernisation blames **internal tradition**; dependency blames **external exploitation**.

#### ii) The argument
Each theory offers a partial account of women's subordination. Modernisation explains how **culture** limits women and how education and cities can free them, but misses **structures**. Dependency explains how **global exploitation** keeps poor women poor, but misses **patriarchy at home**. A gender analysis needs both, plus attention to power inside households.

### 2. Modernisation theory

#### i) The claims
Modernisation theory, most famously **W. W. Rostow**'s *The Stages of Economic Growth* (==1960==), held that every society passes through five stages — **traditional society, preconditions for take-off, take-off, drive to maturity and high mass consumption**. Poor countries are poor because they are stuck in "traditional" culture; they develop through **industry, cities, education and Western values**.

#### ii) What it says about women
Tradition — purdah, early marriage, large families — oppresses women; **modernity frees** them. The benefits of growth will **trickle down** to women through their husbands.

### 3. Relevance of modernisation to gender equality

#### i) What it gets right
There is truth here. **Girls' education**, **urbanisation**, **mass media** and **paid work** have widened women's choices in many countries. Education delays marriage, lowers fertility and raises women's earnings. Exposure to media and city life can change ideas about what women may do — for example, acceptance of women working as teachers, doctors or police officers.

#### ii) Real cultural obstacles
Some cultural practices — **child marriage**, **honour killing**, **denial of inheritance** — are real barriers to equality, and changing attitudes matters.

### 4. The gender critique of modernisation

#### i) Boserup's evidence
**Ester Boserup** (*Woman's Role in Economic Development*, ==1970==) showed that modernising agriculture often **marginalised** women: land titles, machines, training and credit went to men, so women farmers lost income and status.

#### ii) Blaming culture
By blaming "tradition", the theory ignored **colonial history, land ownership, trade and state policy**. It also assumed the West was a model of equality — which it was not.

### 5. Dependency theory

#### i) The claims
**Raúl Prebisch**, **Andre Gunder Frank** ("the development of underdevelopment", ==1966==) and **Walter Rodney** argued that poor countries were **made poor** by colonialism and unequal trade: the rich **centre** extracts raw materials, cheap labour and profits from the **periphery**. Underdevelopment is not an early stage but a **result**.

#### ii) What it says about women
Very little directly: dependency theory spoke of **nations and classes**, not women and men. Its cure — national self-reliance and industrialisation — imagined a male industrial worker as the hero of development, while women's farm and household work stayed invisible.

### 6. Relevance of dependency to gender equality

#### i) Cheap female labour
Feminists used dependency ideas to explain why women in the periphery do the **lowest-paid** work in global chains — garment stitching, cotton picking, home-based piece work.

#### ii) Debt and services
Dependence on foreign lenders brings **austerity**, which cuts the health, education and social services women depend on most.

#### iii) Structures, not culture
It moves the explanation of poor women's lives from their "culture" to the **global economy**.

### 7. The gender critique of dependency

#### i) Gender-blind
It ignored the gender division of labour inside the periphery.

#### ii) Local patriarchy ignored
By blaming the centre, it overlooked **local** structures — landlords, male household heads, customary law — that subordinate women whatever the world system.

#### iii) Unpaid work
It missed how women's **unpaid subsistence and care work** subsidises cheap exports (**Maria Mies**, ==1986==).

::: fig compare | The two theories and women's subordination.
## Modernisation
- Cause: internal tradition
- Cure: education, cities, Western values
- Explains: cultural barriers to women
- Misses: structures, colonial history, men's capture of resources
## Dependency
- Cause: external exploitation
- Cure: break unequal ties, self-reliance
- Explains: cheap female labour, debt, weak services
- Misses: local patriarchy, unpaid work
:::

### 8. Subordination in modern societies — through modernisation's lens

#### i) Modernity did not end patriarchy
Modern societies created **new forms** of subordination: the **double burden** of paid work plus housework; the **glass ceiling**; the **gender pay gap**; and beauty and consumer industries that commodify women's bodies.

#### ii) What the lens explains
Even in rich countries, where "traditional" attitudes persist inside modern institutions — for example, the belief that men are breadwinners — women are paid less and promoted less. Modernisation theory explains these as **cultural lag**, but cannot explain why they persist in the richest countries. The persistence suggests that subordination is built into **structures** — labour markets that penalise carers, and the unequal division of unpaid work — not only into attitudes.

### 9. Subordination through dependency's lens

#### i) Women at the bottom of global chains
In modern, globalised economies, women in poorer countries are drawn into **export factories** and **home-based work** on insecure terms — the **Rana Plaza** collapse in Bangladesh (==2013==) killed more than 1,100 garment workers, most of them women.

#### ii) Austerity and care
Debt and adjustment shift costs from the state to **women's unpaid care**.

### 10. What both theories miss

#### i) Power inside the household
Both treat the household as a harmonious unit. Feminist economists — **Amartya Sen**'s "cooperative conflict" (==1990==) and **Bina Agarwal**'s bargaining model — show that women often lose in household bargaining over income, food and decisions.

#### ii) Unpaid care
Neither counted the unpaid work that keeps economies running.

#### iii) Women's agency
Both treat women as passive — victims of tradition or of global capital — rather than as actors who organise and resist, as women workers' unions and rural women's movements show.

### 11. Pakistan and conclusion

#### i) Pakistan
Modernisation is visible in rising **girls' enrolment** and falling fertility; dependency in Pakistan's reliance on **textile exports** and repeated **IMF programmes**. Yet female labour-force participation remains ==24.4%==, and Pakistan ranked ==143rd of 145== in the Global Gender Gap Report 2026 — showing that neither modernisation nor escape from dependency alone will produce equality.

#### ii) Conclusion
Modernisation theory is relevant to gender equality because **culture and education** matter; dependency theory is relevant because **global structures** matter. But women's subordination in modern societies also rests on **household power, unpaid care and male control of resources** — which neither theory saw. A gender analysis joins the **cultural** and the **structural**, the **local** and the **global** — and asks, in every case, who does the work, who owns the resources and who decides.

## Why this answer scores
- **Both limbs** answered: relevance of each theory, then analysis of subordination through each lens.
- Each theory gets its **claims, relevance and gender critique** with named scholars and dates.
- A **compare figure** shows what each explains and misses.
- The answer adds **what both miss** — household bargaining and unpaid care — raising it above description.
